{
  "_note": "Third layer: injected spot effects the script places by name between the two halves of a line it splits (see prompts/analyze.txt rule 12). Foreground contract I=-20 LUFS / TP=-3 dBTP — voice territory, not the -26 bed contract. **`mode` and `hold` live here, per sound, and are NOT written in the script**: how a clip behaves is a property of the clip (a splat punctuates, a rustle runs under, a bed keeps going), and a per-chapter copy of it was a second source of truth the analyzer had to guess. `hit` holds the whole clip as silence, `overlap` costs no timeline time and runs under the following speech, `trail` holds `hold` seconds solo then tails under. `level` is an optional trim (default 1.0). Sparse transients (coin, swoosh, wood-chop) read several LU low on integrated loudness; that number does not describe them, their peaks sit with the voice peaks. The ceiling is applied to the sample peak before encoding, and a 64k mono mp3 overshoots on a transient, so the reconstructed true peak reads up to ~1.6 dB above -3 dBTP on the shortest takes (swoosh, sword-slash-3/4) while the sample peak sits on it. looped=true on beds only (kitchen beds, footstep loops): a bed the script s is filled by repeating the clip with a short crossfade at each seam (loop_xfade_s), so its length follows the scene rather than the take. Every one-shot stays false — a coin plays once. A bed with no  has no window to fill and plays once too, which is why the digest has to place the stop. dur_s is the longest take, post-trim — the prompt renders it so the analyzer never `hit`s a 51 s boil.",
